00:11:29,280 --> 00:11:31,780
It's called
"roshambo" in Chinese.

251
00:11:31,780 --> 00:11:33,810
It's a very popular game
throughout the world.

252
00:11:33,810 --> 00:11:38,790
It's a stochastic game
where each side can

253
00:11:38,790 --> 00:11:43,288
pick a particular
strategy and the state--

254
00:11:43,288 --> 00:11:44,830
you can think of
there being a state,

255
00:11:44,830 --> 00:11:46,140
you could keep track of
what your opponent has

256
00:11:46,140 --> 00:11:47,350
done over time.

257
00:11:47,350 --> 00:11:51,900
So think for a second about
whether a deterministic policy

258
00:11:51,900 --> 00:11:56,950
can be optimal if you're
playing this game repeatedly.

259
00:11:56,950 --> 00:11:59,320
So raise your hand if
a deterministic policy

260
00:11:59,320 --> 00:12:00,120
can be optimal.

261
00:12:02,820 --> 00:12:06,820
Raise your hand if you think a
stochastic policy is optimal.

262
00:12:06,820 --> 00:12:09,030
OK, someone who said
stochastic explain why.

263
00:12:12,210 --> 00:12:13,176
Yes.

264
00:12:13,176 --> 00:12:16,530
[INAUDIBLE] is like circular.

265
00:12:16,530 --> 00:12:20,348
There's no [? 1 ?] [? plus ?]
[? 1 ?] [INAUDIBLE]

266
00:12:20,348 --> 00:12:20,890
That's right.

267
00:12:20,890 --> 00:12:22,420
Yeah, so there's no best--

268
00:12:22,420 --> 00:12:24,060
there's nothing that
strictly dominates

269
00:12:24,060 --> 00:12:25,102
all the other strategies.

270
00:12:25,102 --> 00:12:27,180
And also, if you're
deterministic, what can

271
00:12:27,180 --> 00:12:29,400
your opponent do?

272
00:12:29,400 --> 00:12:32,560
Like, if I say I'm always
going to pick paper,

273
00:12:32,560 --> 00:12:35,390
what does my opponent do?

274
00:12:35,390 --> 00:12:38,790
Yeah, they're always going
to pick the other one,

275
00:12:38,790 --> 00:12:40,040
like rock [? to ?] [? beat. ?]

276
00:12:40,040 --> 00:12:42,170
So anything you do
that's deterministic

277
00:12:42,170 --> 00:12:43,750
can be exploited
by your opponent

278
00:12:43,750 --> 00:12:46,050
if you are playing repeatedly.

279
00:12:46,050 --> 00:12:49,400
And so the optimal thing to
do here is to be stochastic.

280
00:12:49,400 --> 00:12:51,810
So the optimal policy has
to be stochastic here.

281
00:12:51,810 --> 00:12:54,200
Otherwise, all
deterministic policies

282
00:12:54,200 --> 00:12:58,590
are strictly dominated by
good stochastic policy.

283
00:12:58,590 --> 00:13:00,440
And now, you might
think, all right, well,

284
00:13:00,440 --> 00:13:03,130
that sounds different than
what we've seen so far.

285
00:13:03,130 --> 00:13:06,820
But one of the challenges here
is the system is not Markov.

286
00:13:06,820 --> 00:13:10,513
So it's not stochastic what
your adversary will play next.

287
00:13:10,513 --> 00:13:12,430
They're not random-- or
it might be if they're

288
00:13:12,430 --> 00:13:13,510
playing a stochastic policy.

289
00:13:13,510 --> 00:13:14,593
[CLEARS THROAT] Excuse me.

290
00:13:14,593 --> 00:13:16,120
But in general,
[? they ?] can react

291
00:13:16,120 --> 00:13:17,530
to what you've seen so far.

292
00:13:17,530 --> 00:13:21,220
And it's not just like a random
environment, like a coin flip

293
00:13:21,220 --> 00:13:22,740
on the next time.

294
00:13:22,740 --> 00:13:24,960
And in this case, actually,
a uniform random policy

295
00:13:24,960 --> 00:13:25,960
is optimal.

296
00:13:25,960 --> 00:13:27,310
It's a Nash equilibrium.

297
00:13:30,050 --> 00:13:33,370
So that's one case where
having a stochastic policy

298
00:13:33,370 --> 00:13:34,400
would be really helpful.

299
00:13:34,400 --> 00:13:36,442
So you could just have a
fixed stochastic policy,

300
00:13:36,442 --> 00:13:39,370
and it would be optimal,
but you couldn't necessarily

301
00:13:39,370 --> 00:13:41,140
write this down
easily as a Q-function

302
00:13:41,140 --> 00:13:42,310
and just take the argmax.

303
00:13:42,310 --> 00:13:45,160
There is not a deterministic
policy for this environment

304
00:13:45,160 --> 00:13:46,660
that is optimal.

305
00:13:46,660 --> 00:13:49,960
And so it's less clear how you
would write that down directly

306
00:13:49,960 --> 00:13:52,660
in terms of a Q-function, in
part, because the system is not

307
00:13:52,660 --> 00:13:54,590
Markov.

308
00:13:54,590 --> 00:13:56,480
So here's another example
where we might want

309
00:13:56,480 --> 00:13:57,710
to have stochastic policies.

310
00:13:57,710 --> 00:14:00,950
And it's where we have aliasing
or partial observability.

311
00:14:00,950 --> 00:14:02,870
So imagine this
case where you have

312
00:14:02,870 --> 00:14:04,370
a robot that's walking along.

313
00:14:04,370 --> 00:14:07,040
And maybe they have sensors
so they can tell how far they

314
00:14:07,040 --> 00:14:08,130
are from the walls.

315
00:14:08,130 --> 00:14:13,740
But under those sensors, these
two gray boxes look identical.

316
00:14:13,740 --> 00:14:16,360
Because like from the
agent's point of view,

317
00:14:16,360 --> 00:14:18,750
if they have only immediate
sensors, both of those places

318
00:14:18,750 --> 00:14:20,280
will look identical.

319
00:14:20,280 --> 00:14:23,010
And so they can't distinguish
those gray states.

320
00:14:23,010 --> 00:14:26,370
And imagine that you just--
because you have a feature

321
00:14:26,370 --> 00:14:29,077
representation that just tells
you about what you're-- whether

322
00:14:29,077 --> 00:14:31,410
you have a wall to the north,
to the east, to the south,

323
00:14:31,410 --> 00:14:34,110
or to the west, so those two
gray states would look identical

324
00:14:34,110 --> 00:14:36,510
if that was your
feature representation.

325
00:14:36,510 --> 00:14:39,540
So you could have a value-based
reinforcement learning

326
00:14:39,540 --> 00:14:42,850
representation where you use
an approximate value function,

327
00:14:42,850 --> 00:14:45,870
where you take in this as
the state representation,

328
00:14:45,870 --> 00:14:51,080
or you could have a policy-based
one that takes in those.

329
00:14:51,080 --> 00:14:54,600
So the challenge here is
that if you're value-based,

330
00:14:54,600 --> 00:14:58,460
you have to do the same thing
in those two gray states,

331
00:14:58,460 --> 00:15:00,210
because you can't
distinguish them.

332
00:15:00,210 --> 00:15:03,200
So from your perspective, it's
like you're in the same place

333
00:15:03,200 --> 00:15:05,390
no matter which of those
two places you're in.

334
00:15:05,390 --> 00:15:07,460
So if you're going to do
a value function based,

335
00:15:07,460 --> 00:15:09,840
and then extract a
deterministic policy,

336
00:15:09,840 --> 00:15:12,680
you would either
always have to go

337
00:15:12,680 --> 00:15:15,205
say to the left in those cases
or always go to the right.

338
00:15:17,750 --> 00:15:21,205
And neither of those
would always be good.

339
00:15:24,770 --> 00:15:27,620
So under aliasing,
meaning that we don't know

340
00:15:27,620 --> 00:15:29,150
whether which of
the two gray states

341
00:15:29,150 --> 00:15:30,710
we're in when we're
in one of them,

342
00:15:30,710 --> 00:15:32,600
an optimal deterministic
policy will always

343
00:15:32,600 --> 00:15:35,160
move west in both states
or east in both states.

344
00:15:35,160 --> 00:15:37,160
And either way, it might
get stuck and never

345
00:15:37,160 --> 00:15:40,015
be able to reach the money.

346
00:15:40,015 --> 00:15:41,390
And that's what's
going to happen

347
00:15:41,390 --> 00:15:44,650
if we do a value-based
reinforcement learning approach.

348
00:15:44,650 --> 00:15:45,530
So that's not great.

349
00:15:45,530 --> 00:15:47,447
You're going to traverse
this for a long time.

350
00:15:47,447 --> 00:15:49,450
You're not going to be
getting high reward.

351
00:15:49,450 --> 00:15:52,905
What could you do if you wanted
to have a stochastic policy?

352
00:15:57,270 --> 00:16:01,410
So that allows you to act
randomly or stochastically

353
00:16:01,410 --> 00:16:02,170
in any state.

354
00:16:02,170 --> 00:16:03,510
What do you think would
be the right thing

355
00:16:03,510 --> 00:16:06,270
to do in the gray states if you
could have a stochastic policy?

356
00:16:09,240 --> 00:16:13,420
With just some probability,
you go either east or west.

357
00:16:13,420 --> 00:16:15,630
Yeah, exactly.

358
00:16:15,630 --> 00:16:17,110
So you could just randomize it.

359
00:16:17,110 --> 00:16:21,038
So an optimal stochastic policy
will randomly move east or west

360
00:16:21,038 --> 00:16:23,580
in the gray state, because it
doesn't know which one it's in.

361
00:16:23,580 --> 00:16:27,040
And half the time, that'll
be the right thing to do.

362
00:16:27,040 --> 00:16:30,750
So now that means much more of
the time, it'll go into here.

363
00:16:30,750 --> 00:16:34,860
And it generally will reach
the goal state pretty quickly.

364
00:16:34,860 --> 00:16:38,010
So this is another case where
the system is not Markov.

365
00:16:38,010 --> 00:16:45,640
This is not [INAUDIBLE]
the state features.

366
00:16:45,640 --> 00:16:47,440
So because we have
aliasing, meaning

367
00:16:47,440 --> 00:16:49,250
the system is
partially observable,

368
00:16:49,250 --> 00:16:50,930
it is not a Markov system.

369
00:16:50,930 --> 00:16:52,780
One way to handle
that is to treat it

370
00:16:52,780 --> 00:16:55,090
as a partially observable
Markov decision process.

371
00:16:55,090 --> 00:16:58,690
[INAUDIBLE] talks a lot
about those in his classes.

372
00:16:58,690 --> 00:17:02,740
But an alternative is to
use a stochastic policy.

373
00:17:02,740 --> 00:17:05,550
And you can also
[? act ?] very well here.

374
00:17:05,550 --> 00:17:08,700
So those are two examples
of the type of thing that

375
00:17:08,700 --> 00:17:12,210
might be able to be easy to
handle with policy gradient

376
00:17:12,210 --> 00:17:14,010
methods or stochastic
policies that

377
00:17:14,010 --> 00:17:16,650
might be hard to tackle
with the type of methods

378
00:17:16,650 --> 00:17:18,180
we've seen so far.

379
00:17:18,180 --> 00:17:20,631
So now, we have to think
about if we have policies,

380
00:17:20,631 --> 00:17:23,089
and, in general, we're going
to want them to be stochastic,

381
00:17:23,089 --> 00:17:25,500
how are we going to learn
what are good policies?

382
00:17:25,500 --> 00:17:27,990
Like, we have this-- now,
we have a function space

383
00:17:27,990 --> 00:17:28,950
over policies.

384
00:17:28,950 --> 00:17:32,640
And we want to learn which
of them have good values.

385
00:17:32,640 --> 00:17:35,830
So if we're in an
episodic environment,

386
00:17:35,830 --> 00:17:38,470
we can use the policy
value at the start state.

387
00:17:38,470 --> 00:17:40,950
So we can just say I'm going
to similar to the Monte Carlo

388
00:17:40,950 --> 00:17:42,460
methods, if I start
in this state,

389
00:17:42,460 --> 00:17:45,360
I run this policy, what
would be my expected reward

390
00:17:45,360 --> 00:17:47,430
be until the end of the episode?

391
00:17:47,430 --> 00:17:50,520
We're going to mostly focus
on the episodic case today,

392
00:17:50,520 --> 00:17:55,920
but you can extend these to more
of an infinite horizon case.

393
00:17:55,920 --> 00:17:58,225
All right, so once we
think of it in this way,

394
00:17:58,225 --> 00:18:00,600
we can really think, OK, this
sounds like an optimization

395
00:18:00,600 --> 00:18:01,380
problem.

396
00:18:01,380 --> 00:18:05,670
So we really just want to
find the parameters that

397
00:18:05,670 --> 00:18:07,000
maximize the value.

398
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:09,070
So you could say--

399
00:18:09,070 --> 00:18:11,450
here, you can think of
this as being your thetas.

400
00:18:11,450 --> 00:18:13,220
So I'm just drawing
it in one dimensional.

401
00:18:13,220 --> 00:18:15,310
But in general, this could
be all the parameters

402
00:18:15,310 --> 00:18:16,960
in a deep neural network.

403
00:18:16,960 --> 00:18:22,330
And then, this is V of theta
of a particular starting state.

404
00:18:22,330 --> 00:18:25,560
It might look like this.

405
00:18:25,560 --> 00:18:28,660
And what your goal
would be is to find

406
00:18:28,660 --> 00:18:31,330
the parameters of
your policy that

407
00:18:31,330 --> 00:18:34,200
maximize the value function.

408
00:18:34,200 --> 00:18:36,820
And so this is an
optimization problem,

409
00:18:36,820 --> 00:18:38,430
but it's a hard
optimization problem,

410
00:18:38,430 --> 00:18:40,270
because we don't
have that function.

411
00:18:40,270 --> 00:18:42,978
You can only estimate
it through data.

412
00:18:42,978 --> 00:18:44,770
And so you can imagine,
like you start off,

413
00:18:44,770 --> 00:18:47,650
you have no idea
how theta maps to V.

414
00:18:47,650 --> 00:18:49,400
And then you have to
learn that over time.

415
00:18:54,060 --> 00:18:56,822
So once we think of it as
an optimization problem,

416
00:18:56,822 --> 00:18:58,530
where we don't know
what the function is,

417
00:18:58,530 --> 00:19:00,030
there are a lot of
different methods

418
00:19:00,030 --> 00:19:02,120
we could think about to
try to solve this problem.

419
00:19:02,120 --> 00:19:03,840
And what we're going
to focus on today,

420
00:19:03,840 --> 00:19:06,540
mostly, is ones that are
going to exploit something

421
00:19:06,540 --> 00:19:10,080
about the structure of
sequential decision processes.

422
00:19:10,080 --> 00:19:14,970
But there are methods that
completely ignore all of this.

423
00:19:14,970 --> 00:19:17,520
And in particular, you
can even use things that

424
00:19:17,520 --> 00:19:18,825
completely ignore gradients.

425
00:19:18,825 --> 00:19:21,450
So you can do things like hill
climbing, or genetic algorithms,

426
00:19:21,450 --> 00:19:24,690
or cross entropy methods,
where you may not

427
00:19:24,690 --> 00:19:27,390
think of any of the type of
structure of the parameter

428
00:19:27,390 --> 00:19:28,740
space.

429
00:19:28,740 --> 00:19:32,710
And in some cases, that
can work really well.

430
00:19:32,710 --> 00:19:37,010
So there's a really nice example
by my colleague, Steven Collins,

431
00:19:37,010 --> 00:19:39,370
who's over in the mechanical
engineering department.

432
00:19:39,370 --> 00:19:42,220
He does some really
interesting work on-- oh, yeah.

433
00:19:42,220 --> 00:19:44,320
[INAUDIBLE]

434
00:19:51,470 --> 00:19:55,260
Yes, but you can make it a
distribution over actions.

435
00:19:55,260 --> 00:19:58,010
So you can output
something between 0 and 1.

436
00:19:58,010 --> 00:19:59,652
And you can have
an input action.

437
00:19:59,652 --> 00:20:01,735
And so [INAUDIBLE] [? have ?]
a stochastic policy.

438
00:20:01,735 --> 00:20:03,500
[INAUDIBLE]

439
00:20:04,468 --> 00:20:06,510
You would then compute it
for all of the actions,

440
00:20:06,510 --> 00:20:07,410
then you would have
to have some-- yeah,

441
00:20:07,410 --> 00:20:08,868
you'd have to pick
a random number,

442
00:20:08,868 --> 00:20:10,110
and then use that to select.

443
00:20:10,110 --> 00:20:10,610
Yeah.

444
00:20:10,610 --> 00:20:11,695
Yeah, good question.

445
00:20:15,240 --> 00:20:18,310
So my colleague Steven Collins
over at mechanical engineering

446
00:20:18,310 --> 00:20:20,000
does a lot of work
on exoskeletons.

447
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:21,750
And there are lots of
reasons exoskeletons

448
00:20:21,750 --> 00:20:23,750
could be really helpful,
particularly for people

449
00:20:23,750 --> 00:20:24,930
that have motor impairments.

450
00:20:24,930 --> 00:20:26,430
But one of the
challenges of them

451
00:20:26,430 --> 00:20:30,310
is that you have to have them
actually help people walk.

452
00:20:30,310 --> 00:20:33,670
So if you clamp something
on to, say, your leg,

453
00:20:33,670 --> 00:20:36,405
then the way my physical
configuration is

454
00:20:36,405 --> 00:20:38,530
may not be the same as your
physical configuration.

455
00:20:38,530 --> 00:20:39,450
I'm pretty tall.

456
00:20:39,450 --> 00:20:40,992
And so you'd really
like to make sure

457
00:20:40,992 --> 00:20:43,840
that this helps each individual
in the best way possible.

458
00:20:43,840 --> 00:20:47,580
But you don't want it to have
to learn over the course of two

459
00:20:47,580 --> 00:20:49,710
years how to best
optimize to someone,

460
00:20:49,710 --> 00:20:52,240
because they're not going
to wait that long to use it.

461
00:20:52,240 --> 00:20:54,940
So what they did is
they use policy methods,

462
00:20:54,940 --> 00:20:58,380
policy search methods to quickly
personalize the parameters

463
00:20:58,380 --> 00:21:00,120
of an exoskeleton.

464
00:21:00,120 --> 00:21:02,310
And they called this
human-in-the-loop exoskeleton

465
00:21:02,310 --> 00:21:03,430
optimization.

466
00:21:03,430 --> 00:21:06,360
So the idea in this case is what
they're trying to figure out

467
00:21:06,360 --> 00:21:09,280
is what is the parameters
of their exoskeleton.

468
00:21:09,280 --> 00:21:11,190
And what they're looking
at is, essentially,

469
00:21:11,190 --> 00:21:12,550
how much it helps you walk.

470
00:21:12,550 --> 00:21:15,280
So how much it reduces
the effort needed to walk.

471
00:21:15,280 --> 00:21:17,200
And so what they
could do in this case,

472
00:21:17,200 --> 00:21:19,740
they're not using a
gradient-based method.

473
00:21:19,740 --> 00:21:22,980
They're using just CMA-ES, which
is-- [? so the treatment is ?]

474
00:21:22,980 --> 00:21:24,780
continuous
optimization, is they'd

475
00:21:24,780 --> 00:21:28,930
have people walk under a few
different control parameters.

476
00:21:28,930 --> 00:21:31,690
They would see which of those
seem to be most effective,

477
00:21:31,690 --> 00:21:33,520
and then they would
move the policies

478
00:21:33,520 --> 00:21:36,780
they try in that direction
with some stochasticity.

479
00:21:36,780 --> 00:21:40,270
And I think it was within maybe
two or three hours using this,

480
00:21:40,270 --> 00:21:42,760
they could find substantially
better policies.

481
00:21:42,760 --> 00:21:44,940
I think it increased metabolic
efficiency like maybe

482
00:21:44,940 --> 00:21:47,170
by 20% or 30%.

483
00:21:47,170 --> 00:21:48,390
It was pretty remarkable.

484
00:21:48,390 --> 00:21:49,890
And so this was
published in Science

485
00:21:49,890 --> 00:21:51,788
about seven or eight years ago.

486
00:21:51,788 --> 00:21:53,580
But that's another
example of a place where

487
00:21:53,580 --> 00:21:55,975
you can do this sort
of online optimization,

488
00:21:55,975 --> 00:21:57,600
but you don't
necessarily have to think

489
00:21:57,600 --> 00:22:00,438
about the temporal
structure of the policy.

490
00:22:00,438 --> 00:22:01,800
Can I ask a question?

491
00:22:01,800 --> 00:22:02,340
Yeah.

492
00:22:02,340 --> 00:22:06,810
[INAUDIBLE] [? --with ?]
a default policy,

493
00:22:06,810 --> 00:22:09,100
and then try to improve
that default policies?

494
00:22:09,100 --> 00:22:09,768
Great question.

495
00:22:09,768 --> 00:22:11,310
Yeah, so in all of
these cases, we're

496
00:22:11,310 --> 00:22:13,518
going to have to assume that
we initialize our policy

497
00:22:13,518 --> 00:22:15,510
parameterization in some way.

498
00:22:15,510 --> 00:22:18,060
Just like how we initialized
our value function to 0

499
00:22:18,060 --> 00:22:20,283
to start, now we're going
to-- or if you had it

500
00:22:20,283 --> 00:22:22,950
for the deep Q-network, it would
be whatever your neural network

501
00:22:22,950 --> 00:22:24,630
parameters were.

502
00:22:24,630 --> 00:22:25,505
Yeah, great question.

503
00:22:29,370 --> 00:22:31,530
Now, it's just useful
to know about these,

504
00:22:31,530 --> 00:22:34,410
because they often
work pretty well.

505
00:22:34,410 --> 00:22:37,290
So I think sometimes
we like to leverage

506
00:22:37,290 --> 00:22:40,180
the structure specific to, say,
our Markov decision process.

507
00:22:40,180 --> 00:22:42,330
But in some cases, just
leveraging these ones,

508
00:22:42,330 --> 00:22:44,350
which may not use very
much structure at all,

509
00:22:44,350 --> 00:22:46,710
can actually do really well.

510
00:22:46,710 --> 00:22:48,240
So it's just good
to keep in mind

511
00:22:48,240 --> 00:22:52,310
that there are a lot of ways
to do online optimization.

512
00:22:52,310 --> 00:22:54,600
All right, so this is often
a great baseline to try.

513
00:22:54,600 --> 00:22:57,140
The great thing about this is
it can work with any policy

514
00:22:57,140 --> 00:23:00,590
parameterizations, even if it's
not differentiable, because it's

515
00:23:00,590 --> 00:23:01,710
not using gradients.

516
00:23:01,710 --> 00:23:03,480
So it doesn't need
to be differentiable.

517
00:23:03,480 --> 00:23:06,510
And it's also often very
easy to parallelize.

518
00:23:06,510 --> 00:23:10,790
So CMA-ES, for those of you
who haven't seen it before,

519
00:23:10,790 --> 00:23:12,917
you'll have a number
of different policies

520
00:23:12,917 --> 00:23:14,750
you kind of try in
parallel, and then you'll

521
00:23:14,750 --> 00:23:18,480
use that to update and shift
to another set of policies.

522
00:23:18,480 --> 00:23:21,320
And that's what they did,
Professor Collins did.

523
00:23:21,320 --> 00:23:23,900
And in a lot of cases, the
problems that we think about

524
00:23:23,900 --> 00:23:26,370
are places where you'll
have many customers.

525
00:23:26,370 --> 00:23:29,720
You'll have many robot arms, and
so you can parallelize things.

526
00:23:29,720 --> 00:23:32,360
One of the limitations is that
it's often less data efficient,

527
00:23:32,360 --> 00:23:34,610
because it's ignoring
the temporal structure.

528
00:23:34,610 --> 00:23:36,890
So if you have temporal
structure or if you have

529
00:23:36,890 --> 00:23:39,470
a [? gradient ?] information,
it may be more effective to use

530
00:23:39,470 --> 00:23:43,060
[? that. ?]

531
00:23:43,060 --> 00:23:45,670
So what we're going to
focus on in this class

532
00:23:45,670 --> 00:23:48,170
is differentiable methods.

533
00:23:48,170 --> 00:23:49,810
So we're going to
focus on places where

534
00:23:49,810 --> 00:23:52,780
we can do stochastic
gradient descent,

535
00:23:52,780 --> 00:23:55,330
including on the
policy parametrization.

536
00:23:55,330 --> 00:23:57,490
So if we have our
policy parameterized

537
00:23:57,490 --> 00:24:00,580
by a deep neural network, we
can propagate through that

538
00:24:00,580 --> 00:24:03,670
and update those parameters.

539
00:24:03,670 --> 00:24:08,530
So we're going to focus
here mostly on methods

540
00:24:08,530 --> 00:24:11,740
that do use gradient descent
and that often leverage

541
00:24:11,740 --> 00:24:13,330
the sequential
structure that we're

542
00:24:13,330 --> 00:24:16,120
making a sequence of decisions
and we want to optimize to make

543
00:24:16,120 --> 00:24:19,700
those sequence of decisions.

544
00:24:19,700 --> 00:24:23,150
So to do that, we're going to
explicitly define the gradient.

545
00:24:23,150 --> 00:24:25,820
And we're going to write down
the value function in terms--

546
00:24:25,820 --> 00:24:28,160
as a function of the
policy parameters,

547
00:24:28,160 --> 00:24:34,090
so that we can be clear that
this value function relies

548
00:24:34,090 --> 00:24:35,770
on those policies.

549
00:24:35,770 --> 00:24:38,890
And we're going to focus today
on episodic Markov decision

550
00:24:38,890 --> 00:24:41,210
processes, where we go
for a single episode,

551
00:24:41,210 --> 00:24:42,630
stop, reset, and keep going.

552
00:24:46,450 --> 00:24:48,058
So now, what we're
going to do is

553
00:24:48,058 --> 00:24:49,600
we're only going to
be trying to get,

554
00:24:49,600 --> 00:24:52,660
in general, to a local maximum.

555
00:24:52,660 --> 00:24:56,250
Now, it's possible you're
lucky and you're sort of convex

556
00:24:56,250 --> 00:24:59,080
in the space of the value--

557
00:24:59,080 --> 00:25:00,740
in space of the
policy parameters.

558
00:25:00,740 --> 00:25:03,210
But in general, we're not
going to assume convexity.

559
00:25:03,210 --> 00:25:05,260
So at best, we're going
to hope to just get

560
00:25:05,260 --> 00:25:08,720
to some sort of local
maxima in our space.

561
00:25:08,720 --> 00:25:10,080
So if we have--

562
00:25:10,080 --> 00:25:12,440
again, if we only
had one parameter,

563
00:25:12,440 --> 00:25:17,065
and we have something like
this, we might get to here.

564
00:25:17,065 --> 00:25:17,940
We might get to here.

565
00:25:17,940 --> 00:25:20,148
In general, we're not going
to make global optimality

566
00:25:20,148 --> 00:25:21,470
guarantees.

567
00:25:21,470 --> 00:25:25,620
This is in big contrast to the
tabular cases we saw before.

568
00:25:25,620 --> 00:25:28,910
We were guaranteed to get to
the optimal Q-function, optimal

569
00:25:28,910 --> 00:25:29,920
value function.

570
00:25:29,920 --> 00:25:31,760
Now, we're just
going to hope to--

571
00:25:31,760 --> 00:25:34,400
given our policy
parameterization,

572
00:25:34,400 --> 00:25:38,870
let's try to get to what's a
local optima in that policy

573
00:25:38,870 --> 00:25:40,960
parameterization.

574
00:25:40,960 --> 00:25:43,330
So it's sort of a policy
specific-- policy class

575
00:25:43,330 --> 00:25:45,020
specific guarantee.

576
00:25:45,020 --> 00:25:46,858
And it's only a local optima.

577
00:25:46,858 --> 00:25:48,400
And what we'll be
doing is we're just

578
00:25:48,400 --> 00:25:50,740
going to be trying to take
the gradient of the policy

579
00:25:50,740 --> 00:25:53,310
with respect to the parameters.

580
00:25:53,310 --> 00:25:55,960
And as usual, we're going to
have a step size parameter.

581
00:25:55,960 --> 00:25:58,350
So we're going to take the
gradient of the value function

582
00:25:58,350 --> 00:26:01,050
with respect to the parameters
and take a small step.

583
00:26:01,050 --> 00:26:02,880
And the key thing is
going to be thinking

584
00:26:02,880 --> 00:26:06,630
about places where we can
do this all directly using

585
00:26:06,630 --> 00:26:08,500
smooth functions.

586
00:26:12,256 --> 00:26:14,250
Now, one way you could
do this-- of course,

587
00:26:14,250 --> 00:26:15,710
when you see this
now, you probably

588
00:26:15,710 --> 00:26:17,252
immediately think
of autodiff methods

589
00:26:17,252 --> 00:26:19,590
and think about we can just
back propagate, et cetera.

590
00:26:19,590 --> 00:26:22,400
But it's worth noting that
when these methods began

591
00:26:22,400 --> 00:26:25,280
to start to get popular,
they didn't necessarily

592
00:26:25,280 --> 00:26:27,410
have autodiff yet.

593
00:26:27,410 --> 00:26:27,980
I know.

594
00:26:27,980 --> 00:26:30,350
There was research then still.

595
00:26:30,350 --> 00:26:31,850
And one of the
things people started

596
00:26:31,850 --> 00:26:35,100
thinking about for this is how
you could use this for robotics.

597
00:26:35,100 --> 00:26:38,420
So this is a nice paper
from 2004, so 20 years ago,

598
00:26:38,420 --> 00:26:40,047
by Peter Stone's group.

599
00:26:40,047 --> 00:26:41,130
It was right around then--

600
00:26:41,130 --> 00:26:44,720
I think maybe RoboCop was maybe
60 years old then or something.

601
00:26:44,720 --> 00:26:47,248
I think they started
it back in 1998 or so.

602
00:26:47,248 --> 00:26:49,040
So there are these
little quadruped robots.

603
00:26:49,040 --> 00:26:51,680
And the goal was to think
about getting robotics

604
00:26:51,680 --> 00:26:55,740
to the stage where you could
have robots play human players.

605
00:26:55,740 --> 00:26:58,470
I think that was the goal
by either 2030 or 2050.

606
00:26:58,470 --> 00:26:58,970
I forget.

607
00:26:58,970 --> 00:27:01,637
But what they were just going to
start with-- it was quadrupeds.

608
00:27:01,637 --> 00:27:03,620
So one of the big
challenges at the beginning,

609
00:27:03,620 --> 00:27:05,840
because everywhere, you start
with the beginning challenges,

610
00:27:05,840 --> 00:27:07,370
and you go from
there, it was just

611
00:27:07,370 --> 00:27:09,210
getting them to
walk fast enough.

612
00:27:09,210 --> 00:27:10,860
So if they're going
to score goals,

613
00:27:10,860 --> 00:27:13,450
and they're going to compete,
you need them to walk quickly.

614
00:27:13,450 --> 00:27:15,360
And so there's this
question of just how

615
00:27:15,360 --> 00:27:19,110
do you learn fast walks, so
that they can-- sort of trying

616
00:27:19,110 --> 00:27:21,030
to teach robots to run.

617
00:27:21,030 --> 00:27:24,720
And what they found here is that
they could use policy methods

618
00:27:24,720 --> 00:27:27,480
and policy search methods just
to learn a faster way for it

619
00:27:27,480 --> 00:27:28,300
to walk.

620
00:27:28,300 --> 00:27:31,410
And so they
parameterize the curve

621
00:27:31,410 --> 00:27:34,050
of how the foot moves
as a set of parameters.

622
00:27:34,050 --> 00:27:37,140
And that defined the policy
for moving those joints.

623
00:27:37,140 --> 00:27:38,640
And then, what they
did is they just

624
00:27:38,640 --> 00:27:41,238
had these walk back and
forth many, many times.

625
00:27:41,238 --> 00:27:42,780
And what they would
do is they'd have

626
00:27:42,780 --> 00:27:47,250
them walk with some
particular policy parameters.

627
00:27:47,250 --> 00:27:48,880
They would see how
fast they walked.

628
00:27:48,880 --> 00:27:50,935
They would do finite
different methods.

629
00:27:50,935 --> 00:27:52,560
So they weren't trying
to explicitly do

630
00:27:52,560 --> 00:27:54,720
autodiff or anything
there, and then they

631
00:27:54,720 --> 00:27:59,180
would slightly change the
policy parameters and repeat.

632
00:27:59,180 --> 00:28:02,420
And they learned to
substantially faster walk

633
00:28:02,420 --> 00:28:03,320
during that time.

634
00:28:03,320 --> 00:28:06,750
And I think it took maybe
around four hours or so,

635
00:28:06,750 --> 00:28:10,360
but they just had to replace
the batteries a couple of times.

636
00:28:10,360 --> 00:28:13,010
So just an example to say like,
it's lovely to have autodiff.

637
00:28:13,010 --> 00:28:14,720
You can do really
complicated things now.

638
00:28:14,720 --> 00:28:17,420
But these methods can work
even in really basic settings,

639
00:28:17,420 --> 00:28:20,680
particularly where you think you
have pretty bad models of how

640
00:28:20,680 --> 00:28:21,650
the world works.

641
00:28:21,650 --> 00:28:24,050
And so now, you can just
be directly data driven.

642
00:28:24,050 --> 00:28:26,180
And why is this hard
problem, for those of you

643
00:28:26,180 --> 00:28:28,040
who haven't done robotics?

644
00:28:28,040 --> 00:28:31,510
It involves a whole
bunch of contact forces.

645
00:28:31,510 --> 00:28:33,400
The ground may be--

646
00:28:33,400 --> 00:28:36,030
well, they have to learn
on this particular ground.

647
00:28:36,030 --> 00:28:38,570
You may not know, because
it's commercial hardware.

648
00:28:38,570 --> 00:28:40,540
You may not know exactly
all the parameters

649
00:28:40,540 --> 00:28:42,130
that the designers put in.

650
00:28:42,130 --> 00:28:44,270
So you can just be data driven.

651
00:28:44,270 --> 00:28:49,040
OK, as opposed to maybe
having a physics simulator.

652
00:28:49,040 --> 00:28:50,820
All right, so just
to summarize so far,

653
00:28:50,820 --> 00:28:52,610
the benefits of
policy-based RL is

654
00:28:52,610 --> 00:28:56,548
that we're going to have
often better convergence

655
00:28:56,548 --> 00:28:59,090
properties, because we're often
going to be able to guarantee

656
00:28:59,090 --> 00:29:00,570
that we get to a local optima.

657
00:29:00,570 --> 00:29:03,242
Whereas, we didn't have
that for deep Q-learning.

658
00:29:03,242 --> 00:29:05,450
They're often really effective
in high dimensional or

659
00:29:05,450 --> 00:29:07,350
continuous action spaces.

660
00:29:07,350 --> 00:29:10,070
And you can learn
stochastic policies.

661
00:29:10,070 --> 00:29:11,750
But the methods
we've seen so far

662
00:29:11,750 --> 00:29:14,730
might be more inefficient
and higher variance.

663
00:29:14,730 --> 00:29:18,470
And we often only get to
something in the local optima.

664
00:29:18,470 --> 00:29:21,560
And we'll see some things to
help with of the inefficiency

665
00:29:21,560 --> 00:29:22,090
in a second.

666
00:29:24,270 --> 00:29:26,270
All right, so now, what
we're going to dive into

667
00:29:26,270 --> 00:29:28,280
is how do we do this when
we are willing to have

668
00:29:28,280 --> 00:29:29,350
differentiable policies.

669
00:29:34,400 --> 00:29:37,760
So the hope is that we can
actually compute the policy

670
00:29:37,760 --> 00:29:40,820
gradient analytically,
so we don't have to do it

671
00:29:40,820 --> 00:29:42,500
with finite differences.

672
00:29:42,500 --> 00:29:45,320
And we're going to
focus on policies

673
00:29:45,320 --> 00:29:48,178
where it's differentiable
as long as it's non-0.

674
00:29:48,178 --> 00:29:49,970
So we're going to assume
that we can always

675
00:29:49,970 --> 00:29:53,518
compute the gradient of the
policy parameters themselves.

676
00:29:53,518 --> 00:29:55,310
And there are a number
of different classes

677
00:29:55,310 --> 00:29:56,580
we can do this for.

678
00:29:56,580 --> 00:29:59,760
And there are many popular
classes, including, of course,

679
00:29:59,760 --> 00:30:02,510
deep neural networks.

680
00:30:02,510 --> 00:30:06,600
So popular ones
are often softmax.

681
00:30:06,600 --> 00:30:07,860
Softmax is used all the time.

682
00:30:07,860 --> 00:30:09,235
I'll explain what
is in a second.

683
00:30:09,235 --> 00:30:11,855
Gaussian and neural networks.

684
00:30:11,855 --> 00:30:13,230
And again, just
to be clear here,

685
00:30:13,230 --> 00:30:15,680
what I mean by a
policy class is what

686
00:30:15,680 --> 00:30:17,570
is the functional
form we are using

687
00:30:17,570 --> 00:30:25,050
to give us a probability
of an action given a state.

688
00:30:25,050 --> 00:30:27,110
So are we having
something like-- well, I

689
00:30:27,110 --> 00:30:28,735
guess we can just
see on the next slide

690
00:30:28,735 --> 00:30:30,070
what this will look like.

691
00:30:32,733 --> 00:30:34,650
So we're going to assume
I'm going to give you

692
00:30:34,650 --> 00:30:38,535
some examples of those of
what softmax, and Gaussian,

693
00:30:38,535 --> 00:30:40,740
and neural networks look
like in a second in terms

694
00:30:40,740 --> 00:30:42,720
of how we differentiate them.

695
00:30:42,720 --> 00:30:44,503
But these are just
different ways for us

696
00:30:44,503 --> 00:30:46,920
to parameterize what is the
probability of an action given

697
00:30:46,920 --> 00:30:48,827
a state.

698
00:30:48,827 --> 00:30:51,160
Actually, I guess I'll give
a quick example of Gaussian.

699
00:30:51,160 --> 00:30:52,730
For a Gaussian,
you could imagine--

700
00:30:52,730 --> 00:30:54,190
let's imagine I have a robot.

701
00:30:54,190 --> 00:30:59,020
And I'm trying to figure out,
say, how much speed to apply.

702
00:30:59,020 --> 00:31:00,880
Then, you might
have a policy class

703
00:31:00,880 --> 00:31:06,430
that says the action I take is
equal to a Gaussian centered

704
00:31:06,430 --> 00:31:11,680
around 0.5 with some
standard deviation.

705
00:31:11,680 --> 00:31:14,500
So it would be a
stochastic policy.

706
00:31:14,500 --> 00:31:17,183
And it would say the
average amount of speed

707
00:31:17,183 --> 00:31:18,850
you're going to apply
is 0.5, but you're

708
00:31:18,850 --> 00:31:21,440
going to have some
variability around that.

709
00:31:21,440 --> 00:31:24,490
That would give you some
stochastic behavior.

710
00:31:24,490 --> 00:31:26,490
So sometimes, your robot
would go really slowly.

711
00:31:26,490 --> 00:31:27,657
Sometimes, it would go fast.

712
00:31:27,657 --> 00:31:31,070
Sometimes, it would go
in a negative direction.

713
00:31:31,070 --> 00:31:34,310
OK, so let's keep assuming that
the policy is differentiable.

714
00:31:34,310 --> 00:31:36,390
Whenever it's non-0,
we know the gradient.

715
00:31:36,390 --> 00:31:40,220
That still doesn't tell us how
to solve policy gradient methods

716
00:31:40,220 --> 00:31:43,100
yet, because what we want to do
is take derivatives of the value

717
00:31:43,100 --> 00:31:43,970
function.

718
00:31:43,970 --> 00:31:47,720
So we want to say I want to find
the maximum, the policy that

719
00:31:47,720 --> 00:31:49,160
has the best value
function, which

720
00:31:49,160 --> 00:31:51,618
means I'm going to need to take
the derivative of the value

721
00:31:51,618 --> 00:31:55,322
function with respect to
the policy parameters.

722
00:31:55,322 --> 00:31:57,750
So remember that
the policy value,

723
00:31:57,750 --> 00:32:00,260
the value of the initial
starting state under a policy,

724
00:32:00,260 --> 00:32:04,410
is going to be the
expected sum of rewards.

725
00:32:04,410 --> 00:32:06,948
We don't have to use
discounting for most of today

726
00:32:06,948 --> 00:32:07,990
if we assume it's finite.

727
00:32:07,990 --> 00:32:11,020
So I'll just say
we're going to assume

728
00:32:11,020 --> 00:32:14,340
we're in the episodic case.

729
00:32:14,340 --> 00:32:15,490
So this is finite.

730
00:32:15,490 --> 00:32:18,670
So no discount
[? counting ?] for now.

731
00:32:21,988 --> 00:32:23,530
So we don't need
discounting for now,

732
00:32:23,530 --> 00:32:25,240
because it's always a finite
length, so we're never

733
00:32:25,240 --> 00:32:26,860
going to have infinite reward.

734
00:32:26,860 --> 00:32:29,560
So the policy value is
just the expected sum

735
00:32:29,560 --> 00:32:31,060
of discounted rewards
when we follow

736
00:32:31,060 --> 00:32:33,280
the policy
parameterized by theta

737
00:32:33,280 --> 00:32:35,830
till the end of the
episode, starting

738
00:32:35,830 --> 00:32:40,620
from the state [? s0. ?] And
there are lots of different ways

739
00:32:40,620 --> 00:32:43,150
for us to write this down.

740
00:32:43,150 --> 00:32:47,350
So one way is for us to write
down [INAUDIBLE] r is equal to--

741
00:32:47,350 --> 00:32:51,310
well, it's equal to the
state action value averaged

742
00:32:51,310 --> 00:32:53,800
over the probability of us
taking each of those actions

