1231
00:57:13,970 --> 00:57:17,980
over what this sort of
gradient estimation is doing.

1232
00:57:21,058 --> 00:57:23,830
So in general, we can also
write down a policy gradient

1233
00:57:23,830 --> 00:57:27,250
theorem, which says, we
could either use something

1234
00:57:27,250 --> 00:57:29,700
like episodic reward.

1235
00:57:29,700 --> 00:57:32,850
Or we could be trying to look
at average reward per time step.

1236
00:57:32,850 --> 00:57:35,350
Or we could be trying to
look at average value.

1237
00:57:35,350 --> 00:57:38,010
And in all of these
cases, we can end up

1238
00:57:38,010 --> 00:57:40,560
writing something that looks
really similar to the equation I

1239
00:57:40,560 --> 00:57:44,250
showed you before, which is
the derivative with respect

1240
00:57:44,250 --> 00:57:46,470
to these value
functions or something

1241
00:57:46,470 --> 00:57:48,900
like a value function is
going to look something

1242
00:57:48,900 --> 00:57:51,990
like the derivative
with the score function,

1243
00:57:51,990 --> 00:57:54,000
the expected value of
the trajectories you're

1244
00:57:54,000 --> 00:57:59,250
going to get, of the log of the
parameters times the Q function

1245
00:57:59,250 --> 00:58:05,180
or the return for that
particular state action

1246
00:58:05,180 --> 00:58:08,060
pair following the policy.

1247
00:58:08,060 --> 00:58:11,660
And there's a nice derivation
in Sutton and Barto about that.

1248
00:58:11,660 --> 00:58:13,910
At a high level, I think the
useful thing to know here

1249
00:58:13,910 --> 00:58:17,690
is just that can extend
it beyond just thinking

1250
00:58:17,690 --> 00:58:20,150
of like the sample of return.

1251
00:58:20,150 --> 00:58:22,460
And we can think of
there being Q functions.

1252
00:58:22,460 --> 00:58:23,100
All right.

1253
00:58:23,100 --> 00:58:27,318
Now what I've shown you so far
is something that is correct,

1254
00:58:27,318 --> 00:58:28,860
and we can turn it
into an algorithm,

1255
00:58:28,860 --> 00:58:31,760
but it does not leverage much
of the temporal structure.

1256
00:58:31,760 --> 00:58:33,360
So what do I mean by that?

1257
00:58:33,360 --> 00:58:36,450
So what we've written down
here is a valid gradient.

1258
00:58:36,450 --> 00:58:39,990
It's unbiased, but
it can be very noisy.

1259
00:58:39,990 --> 00:58:42,650
So we're estimating this
by Monte Carlo method

1260
00:58:42,650 --> 00:58:44,240
because we have these m samples.

1261
00:58:44,240 --> 00:58:46,710
And as we know from Monte
Carlo methods before,

1262
00:58:46,710 --> 00:58:49,910
they are unbiased, but they
can be very high variance.

1263
00:58:49,910 --> 00:58:52,520
And so some of the ways to
make this more practical,

1264
00:58:52,520 --> 00:58:55,490
and what I mean by that is a
better estimate of the gradient

1265
00:58:55,490 --> 00:58:57,930
and hopefully with less
data, because ultimately,

1266
00:58:57,930 --> 00:59:00,290
we're going to have to
be using this information

1267
00:59:00,290 --> 00:59:03,397
to update our weights to
try to get to a good policy.

1268
00:59:03,397 --> 00:59:04,980
So we want this to
be data efficient--

1269
00:59:04,980 --> 00:59:07,022
is we can try to leverage
the temporal structure,

1270
00:59:07,022 --> 00:59:09,500
and we can also
include baselines.

1271
00:59:09,500 --> 00:59:10,110
All right.

1272
00:59:10,110 --> 00:59:12,420
So let's first see the
temporal structure.

1273
00:59:12,420 --> 00:59:16,050
So what we've done before is
we've summed up all the rewards

1274
00:59:16,050 --> 00:59:17,880
from a whole
trajectory, and we've

1275
00:59:17,880 --> 00:59:21,480
multiplied it by the sum
of the score function

1276
00:59:21,480 --> 00:59:22,690
for the whole trajectory.

1277
00:59:22,690 --> 00:59:24,570
That's what we've done so far.

1278
00:59:24,570 --> 00:59:26,790
We can instead
think of it as, what

1279
00:59:26,790 --> 00:59:29,010
if we have the gradient
estimator for a single reward

1280
00:59:29,010 --> 00:59:31,350
term?

1281
00:59:31,350 --> 00:59:34,560
So this is just
for one time step.

1282
00:59:34,560 --> 00:59:36,450
We can think of it
for there, which

1283
00:59:36,450 --> 00:59:40,290
is we have that single
time step times the score

1284
00:59:40,290 --> 00:59:42,870
function for the
remaining time steps

1285
00:59:42,870 --> 00:59:47,208
or for the time steps
up to that point.

1286
00:59:47,208 --> 00:59:49,710
So it's like we just think
of the partial trajectory

1287
00:59:49,710 --> 00:59:50,795
until we got that reward.

1288
00:59:53,410 --> 00:59:56,255
So we want to think about
the derivative of this.

1289
00:59:56,255 --> 00:59:58,130
This is the reward we
got at this time point.

1290
00:59:58,130 --> 00:59:59,630
So instead of having
this whole sum,

1291
00:59:59,630 --> 01:00:02,920
we just think of, well, what is
the trajectory that we got up

1292
01:00:02,920 --> 01:00:05,870
to that time point and all
of their score functions?

1293
01:00:09,465 --> 01:00:10,440
Does that make sense?

1294
01:00:10,440 --> 01:00:12,273
I remember having
questions about that part.

1295
01:00:15,510 --> 01:00:19,350
OK, so this is like for a
single time step t prime.

1296
01:00:19,350 --> 01:00:23,390
And so now what we
can do is we can

1297
01:00:23,390 --> 01:00:25,470
sum this over all time steps.

1298
01:00:25,470 --> 01:00:29,700
So instead of having the sum
of all rewards times this,

1299
01:00:29,700 --> 01:00:32,790
we can say, well, we know
that for one time step,

1300
01:00:32,790 --> 01:00:34,790
it is equal to the expected
value of the reward

1301
01:00:34,790 --> 01:00:38,630
for that time step
times the score

1302
01:00:38,630 --> 01:00:39,990
functions up to that point.

1303
01:00:39,990 --> 01:00:41,510
So let's just
rewrite it like that.

1304
01:00:41,510 --> 01:00:42,890
Now we're just going
to sum over the rewards

1305
01:00:42,890 --> 01:00:44,125
we got for all time steps.

1306
01:00:46,900 --> 01:00:48,370
All right.

1307
01:00:48,370 --> 01:00:53,190
So now what we can do is we
can do slight rearrangement.

1308
01:00:53,190 --> 01:00:57,800
So what we can notice is
that for each of the points,

1309
01:00:57,800 --> 01:01:00,310
so you can think
of it as, I have--

1310
01:01:00,310 --> 01:01:04,810
so this is t, 0, 1, 2, 3.

1311
01:01:04,810 --> 01:01:08,490
And you can think of
all of these score

1312
01:01:08,490 --> 01:01:10,860
functions I have
at each time point.

1313
01:01:10,860 --> 01:01:14,700
So the score function
at time step 0

1314
01:01:14,700 --> 01:01:22,110
is going to appear for r0,
r1, r2, r3, dot, dot dot.

1315
01:01:22,110 --> 01:01:23,680
So that's what I've
just done here.

1316
01:01:23,680 --> 01:01:25,590
I've said this
first term is going

1317
01:01:25,590 --> 01:01:32,330
to appear with a reward function
of all of the subsequent time

1318
01:01:32,330 --> 01:01:34,880
points.

1319
01:01:34,880 --> 01:01:37,390
Because that decision happened,
and then we got a reward,

1320
01:01:37,390 --> 01:01:39,348
and then we got a whole
bunch of rewards later.

1321
01:01:41,880 --> 01:01:44,370
On the first time
step, it can affect

1322
01:01:44,370 --> 01:01:46,230
the reward you
get at time step 1

1323
01:01:46,230 --> 01:01:49,430
and all the feature after that.

1324
01:01:49,430 --> 01:01:54,350
So this term here, this score
function can influence--

1325
01:01:54,350 --> 01:01:55,970
the one that we
get on time step 1

1326
01:01:55,970 --> 01:01:58,460
can influence time
step 1 all the way out

1327
01:01:58,460 --> 01:02:00,110
to the end of the episode.

1328
01:02:00,110 --> 01:02:03,350
The one we get on time step
2 can influence time step 2

1329
01:02:03,350 --> 01:02:06,780
all the way out to the
end of the episode.

1330
01:02:06,780 --> 01:02:08,400
So essentially this
is like saying,

1331
01:02:08,400 --> 01:02:12,180
my reward on time step 3 cannot
be impacted by decisions I make

1332
01:02:12,180 --> 01:02:13,470
on time step 4.

1333
01:02:13,470 --> 01:02:16,380
Time only flows one way.

1334
01:02:16,380 --> 01:02:18,890
So if we think about what
those score functions were

1335
01:02:18,890 --> 01:02:20,640
and like we think of
the trajectories that

1336
01:02:20,640 --> 01:02:23,470
were generated, they're
a temporal structure.

1337
01:02:23,470 --> 01:02:27,240
And so it means that
we cannot have--

1338
01:02:27,240 --> 01:02:30,000
if we change the policy
parameters such that decisions

1339
01:02:30,000 --> 01:02:32,880
in the future change, that
can't affect my reward

1340
01:02:32,880 --> 01:02:34,920
on earlier time steps.

1341
01:02:34,920 --> 01:02:37,620
So this is leveraging
the temporal structure.

1342
01:02:37,620 --> 01:02:42,300
So this just allows us to
rewrite the equation so that now

1343
01:02:42,300 --> 01:02:45,390
we have for each of the
different score functions

1344
01:02:45,390 --> 01:02:49,020
essentially which of the
rewards they influence.

1345
01:02:49,020 --> 01:02:50,880
And the reason this
is important is

1346
01:02:50,880 --> 01:02:54,540
because here you could see
that we're multiplying each

1347
01:02:54,540 --> 01:02:57,780
of the score functions
by all of the rewards,

1348
01:02:57,780 --> 01:02:59,850
and now we're only
going to multiply them

1349
01:02:59,850 --> 01:03:01,925
by the rewards they influence.

1350
01:03:01,925 --> 01:03:04,500
And so in general, that's going
to be way less than having

1351
01:03:04,500 --> 01:03:05,970
the full set of rewards.

1352
01:03:05,970 --> 01:03:08,430
So this is going to reduce
the variance of our estimator

1353
01:03:08,430 --> 01:03:11,550
without causing any bias,
just leveraging the fact

1354
01:03:11,550 --> 01:03:13,860
that decisions in the
future can't affect

1355
01:03:13,860 --> 01:03:14,930
your rewards in the past.

1356
01:03:18,480 --> 01:03:19,180
All right.

1357
01:03:19,180 --> 01:03:21,150
So that is one of
the first things

1358
01:03:21,150 --> 01:03:24,000
that we're going
to do in this case.

1359
01:03:24,000 --> 01:03:26,340
So we're going to write--

1360
01:03:26,340 --> 01:03:29,597
so remember in this case that
if we sum up all the rewards

1361
01:03:29,597 --> 01:03:31,180
from the current
time step to the end,

1362
01:03:31,180 --> 01:03:32,730
we just called that the return.

1363
01:03:32,730 --> 01:03:35,610
We've seen that before
from Monte Carlo.

1364
01:03:35,610 --> 01:03:38,950
So we can just rewrite
this expression like that.

1365
01:03:38,950 --> 01:03:41,860
And that gives us the
reinforce algorithm.

1366
01:03:41,860 --> 01:03:43,330
So this is the
reinforce algorithm

1367
01:03:43,330 --> 01:03:46,510
that has been incredibly
influential in NLP

1368
01:03:46,510 --> 01:03:49,420
and robotics and
many, many areas.

1369
01:03:49,420 --> 01:03:52,030
And so what this says
here is that the way

1370
01:03:52,030 --> 01:03:55,650
we change our parameter
is just our learning rate

1371
01:03:55,650 --> 01:03:58,620
times our score function
times the return

1372
01:03:58,620 --> 01:04:01,643
we got from that time step
till the end of the episode.

1373
01:04:01,643 --> 01:04:03,810
So we still have to wait
till the end of the episode

1374
01:04:03,810 --> 01:04:05,080
to update anything.

1375
01:04:05,080 --> 01:04:06,990
But what happens is
we run a full episode

1376
01:04:06,990 --> 01:04:08,340
with our current policy.

1377
01:04:08,340 --> 01:04:10,650
And then for each
time step, we slightly

1378
01:04:10,650 --> 01:04:14,460
change our policy parameters by
using a learning rate, the score

1379
01:04:14,460 --> 01:04:16,980
function for that time
step plus the return

1380
01:04:16,980 --> 01:04:20,530
we got from that time step
till the end of the episode.

1381
01:04:20,530 --> 01:04:23,140
And then we just step through
that for the whole episode.

1382
01:04:23,140 --> 01:04:25,230
And that's given us
T different updates

1383
01:04:25,230 --> 01:04:28,110
to our policy parameterization.

1384
01:04:28,110 --> 01:04:32,040
And then we just repeat over
and over and over again.

1385
01:04:32,040 --> 01:04:34,620
And what that guarantees
to us is that eventually we

1386
01:04:34,620 --> 01:04:37,980
will land in a local
optima of the value

1387
01:04:37,980 --> 01:04:40,330
function for the policy
parameterization.

1388
01:04:43,841 --> 01:04:46,000
So this is called Monte
Carlo policy gradient

1389
01:04:46,000 --> 01:04:47,330
or known as the reinforce.

1390
01:04:47,330 --> 01:04:53,200
I believe this was in roughly
1992, so about 30 years ago.

1391
01:04:53,200 --> 01:04:56,590
And it's been many, many, many
policy gradient algorithms are

1392
01:04:56,590 --> 01:04:57,405
built on this idea.

1393
01:05:01,580 --> 01:05:03,000
Now, when you're
looking at this,

1394
01:05:03,000 --> 01:05:05,930
you might still be concerned,
from remembering back

1395
01:05:05,930 --> 01:05:08,280
from the Monte Carlo
methods we've covered,

1396
01:05:08,280 --> 01:05:12,650
that this estimate G can
often be pretty high variance.

1397
01:05:12,650 --> 01:05:15,500
So in general, if you're
just directly averaging

1398
01:05:15,500 --> 01:05:18,440
over sample returns, that
might be high variance.

1399
01:05:18,440 --> 01:05:20,510
So one of the next
fixes we can do,

1400
01:05:20,510 --> 01:05:23,450
and we'll get to this
more on Wednesday,

1401
01:05:23,450 --> 01:05:27,150
is to introduce a baseline.

1402
01:05:27,150 --> 01:05:30,095
And I'll just say
the goals here is

1403
01:05:30,095 --> 01:05:31,470
that we're going
to hopefully try

1404
01:05:31,470 --> 01:05:34,140
to converge as quickly as
possible to local optima.

1405
01:05:34,140 --> 01:05:37,290
So we want to
reduce the variance

1406
01:05:37,290 --> 01:05:39,780
over our gradient estimate.

1407
01:05:39,780 --> 01:05:42,390
And so the baseline is going
to allow us to hopefully

1408
01:05:42,390 --> 01:05:45,450
reduce, well, in general,
yes, reduce the variance

1409
01:05:45,450 --> 01:05:47,950
over this estimation process.

1410
01:05:47,950 --> 01:05:55,210
And we'll see two ideas next,
which is introducing a baseline

1411
01:05:55,210 --> 01:05:57,730
and then thinking about an
alternative to the Monte Carlo

1412
01:05:57,730 --> 01:05:59,650
returns.

1413
01:05:59,650 --> 01:06:02,450
So those are the ideas that
we're going to go through next.

1414
01:06:02,450 --> 01:06:04,360
I guess I'll just
do one more thing,

1415
01:06:04,360 --> 01:06:06,433
and we will go through
the proof of it next time.

1416
01:06:06,433 --> 01:06:08,350
So I'll just introduce
the concept of baseline

1417
01:06:08,350 --> 01:06:09,908
and then we'll
prove it next time.

1418
01:06:09,908 --> 01:06:11,950
So the idea in this case
is that we're just going

1419
01:06:11,950 --> 01:06:13,693
to subtract something off.

1420
01:06:13,693 --> 01:06:15,610
And we're going to
subtract something off that

1421
01:06:15,610 --> 01:06:18,490
only depends on the state.

1422
01:06:18,490 --> 01:06:25,180
This only depends on the state.

1423
01:06:25,180 --> 01:06:27,820
OK, so this is not a
function of your policy,

1424
01:06:27,820 --> 01:06:29,290
only depends on the state.

1425
01:06:29,290 --> 01:06:32,200
And it will turn out, and
we'll prove this next time--

1426
01:06:32,200 --> 01:06:35,350
it's pretty elegant-- that for
any choice of something that

1427
01:06:35,350 --> 01:06:38,560
only depends on your state,
the gradient estimator is still

1428
01:06:38,560 --> 01:06:40,210
unbiased.

1429
01:06:40,210 --> 01:06:42,977
So you couldn't subtract
off anything there.

1430
01:06:42,977 --> 01:06:44,560
That is only a
function of your state,

1431
01:06:44,560 --> 01:06:47,350
and you didn't change the
bias of your estimator, which

1432
01:06:47,350 --> 01:06:49,420
is wild.

1433
01:06:49,420 --> 01:06:52,375
And we'll prove that next time.

1434
01:06:52,375 --> 01:06:54,010
But the goal is that
we can hopefully

1435
01:06:54,010 --> 01:06:56,590
reduce the variance of
our estimated gradient

1436
01:06:56,590 --> 01:07:00,210
by subtracting off
the right thing.

1437
01:07:00,210 --> 01:07:03,070
And just intuitively, the way
to think about the baseline

1438
01:07:03,070 --> 01:07:06,850
is that you don't necessarily
just care about whether or not

1439
01:07:06,850 --> 01:07:09,070
the gradient is
positive or negative

1440
01:07:09,070 --> 01:07:12,303
and whether the returns
were good or bad.

1441
01:07:12,303 --> 01:07:14,470
You might care about, well,
how much better or worse

1442
01:07:14,470 --> 01:07:16,510
are these returns
compared to something

1443
01:07:16,510 --> 01:07:17,830
else I could have done?

1444
01:07:17,830 --> 01:07:20,230
Like, I want to know whether
this policy A is better

1445
01:07:20,230 --> 01:07:23,210
than policy B. And maybe both of
them give you positive returns.

1446
01:07:23,210 --> 01:07:25,880
One of them gives you 100
and of them gives you 90,

1447
01:07:25,880 --> 01:07:27,650
but you'd really like
the one with 100.

1448
01:07:27,650 --> 01:07:30,370
So you'd really like to
move your policy parameters

1449
01:07:30,370 --> 01:07:31,870
in the direction
of stuff that is

1450
01:07:31,870 --> 01:07:34,010
better than other alternatives.

1451
01:07:34,010 --> 01:07:35,823
And that's the
idea of a baseline,

1452
01:07:35,823 --> 01:07:37,240
is to say like,
well, maybe I know

1453
01:07:37,240 --> 01:07:39,310
that I could probably
always get like 90

1454
01:07:39,310 --> 01:07:40,790
for this particular state.

1455
01:07:40,790 --> 01:07:43,180
How much better is this
policy for this state compared

1456
01:07:43,180 --> 01:07:45,340
to something I
could do on average?

1457
01:07:45,340 --> 01:07:47,890
And so we're going to
intuitively increase

1458
01:07:47,890 --> 01:07:50,710
the log probability of an action
proportionally to how much

1459
01:07:50,710 --> 01:07:54,910
its returns were better than
expected, where the baseline is

1460
01:07:54,910 --> 01:07:57,400
giving you that expected value.

1461
01:07:57,400 --> 01:07:59,770
And we'll see
formally on Wednesday

1462
01:07:59,770 --> 01:08:01,820
how by doing this
with the baseline,

1463
01:08:01,820 --> 01:08:03,382
it doesn't introduce any bias.

1464
01:08:03,382 --> 01:08:04,840
So it's going to
be one of the ways

1465
01:08:04,840 --> 01:08:07,155
that we're going to
get better gradients.

1466
01:08:07,155 --> 01:08:09,280
The other thing that we're
going to do on Wednesday

1467
01:08:09,280 --> 01:08:12,760
is we're at least going to
start talking about PPO, which

1468
01:08:12,760 --> 01:08:15,940
is part of your homework
2, bless you, which

1469
01:08:15,940 --> 01:08:19,330
is going to involve more ways to
be more efficient and effective

1470
01:08:19,330 --> 01:08:20,779
in the policies that we do.

1471
01:08:20,779 --> 01:08:21,529
